 # Transient Photon Beams
 
-Also called: Time-resolved Photon Beams
+Also called: PTPB, Progressive Spatio-Temporal Beam Density Estimation, Progressive Transient Photon Beams
 
 ## Overview
-Transient Photon Beams is a numerical method for rendering participating media in the transient state, extending the steady-state Photon Beams algorithm by accounting for photon time-of-flight along continuous beam trajectories [S1]. It solves the problem of computing time-resolved radiance in complex scenes by combining the efficiency of density estimation with the temporal accuracy of continuous path parameterization [S1]. Unlike steady-state variants, it explicitly tracks the optical path length to determine arrival times, and unlike pure Monte Carlo transient methods, it avoids high variance by evenly distributing integrated radiance over temporal intervals derived from ray-beam intersections [S1].
+Transient Photon Beams is a numerical method for rendering participating media in the transient state, extending the steady-state Photon Beam algorithm by accounting for photon time-of-flight along continuous beam trajectories [S1, S2]. It solves the time-resolved Radiative Transfer Equation (RTE) by combining photon beam tracing with 2D density estimation to mitigate variance in the temporal domain [S1]. Unlike standard kernel density estimation which remains biased even with infinite photons, the Progressive variant uses a spatio-temporal kernel reduction scheme to converge to the correct solution with finite memory [S2]. It differs from [Photon Beam Diffusion](3768-photon-beam-diffusion.md) by preserving the full directional and temporal structure of light transport rather than diffusing it, and from [Photon Marching](4775-photon-marching.md) by using continuous beam segments for density estimation rather than discrete point interactions [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two distinct passes: a forward beam tracing pass and a backward ray tracing pass with density estimation.
+The method consists of two main phases: Beam Tracing (Pass 1) and Ray Tracing with Density Estimation (Pass 2). A progressive variant iteratively refines the result by reducing kernel bandwidths.
 
